e's a really nice selection of stuff  here i grab this dance cycle  along with a nice walk and run cycle  should be fine for what we're doing  notice that i download the animation  separately by downloading this without  the skin  so when we hit download we can just  select no skin and that just gets us the  animation data without needing to load  the rest  now that we've got our animation data  let's start by creating a few basic  classes  we're going to make a basic character  controller class which is going to  represent a single animated character  in our little world we'll also make  another class  basic character controller input which  will be responsible for listening for  keyboard input and recording keys like  up and down  that kind of thing lastly we'll make a  finite state machine and state class  we'll fill their implementations out in  a second  now you could easily put them all into  one big class but the reason we're not  going with this one  uber class is simple by decoupling these  classes you can in theory drop in  replacements like say if you have the  basic character controller and you want  to make an ai  you can simply drop in an ai input in  place of the keyboard input and voila  you have an ai character it's just neat  and tidy and saves you time in the long  run  so now the basic flow is like this the  basic character controller has an  instance of an input which will  instantiate right here in the  constructor  and it has a finite state machine the  character controller will load the fbx  files associated with  model and animations and then create a  proxy class that will let the finite  state machine  communicate with the controller to  change animations that kind of thing  you could of course just give it direct  access to the basic character controller  but i'll make a proxy class to avoid  cluttering things up  the loading is all pretty simple we load  the main model file which is what these  lines of code do  then we load each of the animations and  we store the animation clip and  animation  action from all of these in a dictionary  called this dot underscore animations  the keyboard input class is pretty  simple all this does is set a few  document listeners up for the key up and  key down events and record the relevant  ones  there's really not much in here beyond  that no special processing of anything  we're mostly just interested in walking  running so we'll record awsd for walking  the space key for doing a special  animation and the shift key for  transitioning from walking to running  the input here will get directly passed  to the finite state machine to be  interpreted  so not much to see here the real guts of  the logic is here  in the finite state machine so quick  aside  finite state machines are a really  simple way of modeling the possible  states and transitions for a character  imagine we've got this character and  we'll model some simple state for it  initially he's in the idle state since  that's kind of like the default  nothing's happening here if you were to  press forward or backward he'd be  walking  so we add this walk state and you  transition to that from the idle state  when the input was forward or backwards  similarly when you're walking and you  stop pressing those keys we transition  back  into the idle state our character isn't  going to have a ton of states  since this is just a tutorial but here's  the final finite state machine where we  have idle  walk run and dance states let's code  this up  so back in the code we have the finite  state machine class  and this has a few public functions  there's the set state function  which first signals the old state that  it's exiting and sets the current state  and then notifies the new state that  it's active  there's also an update function that  gets called every frame and passes the  frame time and input to the currently  active state  now we'll make a character fsm class  which will just be a child  of the general finite state machine and  i just do that so i can add the  characters specific states here like  idle walk run and dance these add state  calls  what we're doing is passing the name of  the state and then the class that will  get instantiated to represent that state  so for instance when we switch to idle  the finite state machine will  instantiate an idle state  instance let's go over the states now  and we can see how they transition from  one state to the next  beginning with the idle state when we  enter the idle state  we want to set the character's animation  to idle so here in the enter function  this is responsible for playing the idle  animation  if there was a previous state like let's  say the character was walking before  all this code here does is tries to  cross fade them so the old one fades out  and the new one fades in  here's an example on the left is where  we do the crossfade so the character  kind of naturally comes to a halt  on the right there's no crossfade so it  kind of abruptly switches from one  animation to the next  you can see that one kind of looks jerky  and unnatural and the other looks pretty  smooth  next in the update function remember  from the diagram that it can transition  to  either a dance state if you press the  space or a walk state if you press  forward or backward  so in the update function here we'll  check the input if they're moving  forward or backward  we set the state to walk and if they  press the space bar  we set the state to dance next let's  look at the dance state  this is a pretty simple state the enter  function is doing two things  first it's playing the animation again  we crossfade like the idol did but we  also set some different parameters  specifically we only want to play this  once so we set  three dot loop once here and clamp when  finished to true  that just pauses the animation right at  the end once it's done  the other thing we do is set an event  listener to wait for the end of the  animation  because once the animation is done we  just set the state back to idle  the dance state doesn't do any updates  it just plays and then exits  moving on to the walk state this is  pretty similar to the idol in the enter  function we start playing the animation  trying to crossfade if there was another  animation before it  there's just one gotcha that i'll walk  through if the previous date was the  running state  we also want to smoothly fade in from  that but the legs might be in a totally  different place since the running  animation might be halfway through  so we don't want to just start the  walking animation  instead we skip ahead in the walking  animation a bit  the same percentage as the running  animation  that way when we start crossfading you  get a super smooth run to walk  let's watch without this on the left is  if we just crossfade which  kinda looks okay but not great depending  on where the legs were the model kind of  stumbles  if you catch it at the right moment it  looks great and seamless but at the  wrong moment  and it's stuttery now on the right we  have the animation synced and it always  looks smooth  doesn't matter which leg is going  forward or where they are in the cycle  we transition smoothly from walk to run  and vice versa  now the update function for the walk  state is pretty straightforward  you check if you're still walking  otherwise revert to idle  and if the shift key is being held down  switch to the run state  and i'll just skim run state quickly  because it's a near carbon copy of walk  state  the only difference here is in the  update function if they're not pressing  the shift key  revert to walk and that's it once you've  done all that  in the basic character controller class  if you add some velocity and direction  you've got a little walking character  it's not perfect but this should get you  started  you can see i can walk around a bit  looks pretty decent for the amount of  work and if i hold down the shift key  you can see breaks out into a little  trot  run around a bit and then once i let go  of the shift key he drops back and do a  walk  and if i let go it stops all together  finally  if i press the space bar he dances a  little jig  you could do so much more with this go  back to miximo and get a walk backwards  animation  or add some jumps attacks whatever suits  your purposes  hope you enjoyed this if you haven't  already subscribe both here and on  twitter  you'll get notifications when new videos  drop and previews on what's coming up  next  i also chat with people and try to get a  sense of what you all want to see next  make sure to like the video and leave a  comment about what you want to see  covered in a future tutorial  the code is all up on github so go ahead  grab it download it do whatever you want  with it  cheers everyone

